    "reading": "Selection refitted on the full training set, then one evaluation on the held-out test split. Descriptive only: a single measurement with no error bar, on rows from the same extraction as the training data."
  },
  "scope": {
    "what_is_shown": "Adding the extracted quantum columns to the raw sensor channels changes a FIXED linear detector (StandardScaler + LogisticRegression). Measured on identical paired folds, so the contrast is paired and the test is corrected for fold overlap.",
    "one_question_only": "There is exactly one endpoint family: hybrid - raw, on two endpoints. It says nothing about the best achievable detector, and it is not a comparison against a nonlinear classical model.",
    "descriptive_ceiling": "hgb_raw (HistGradientBoosting on the raw channels alone) is DESCRIPTIVE and is not an endpoint. When it beats every linear arm - it usually does - the lift is a representation effect under a weak detector, not evidence that the quantum columns carry information the raw channels lack.",
    "selection_is_part_of_the_model": "The `selected_*` arms search for a column combination, and that search is part of the model. It therefore runs INSIDE each training fold by inner cross-validation, and the chosen subset is scored on outer rows the search never saw. Selecting once on all the training data and then cross-validating would inflate the score, because every fold would already have seen the selection.",
    "matched_search_width": "A search over the classical channels alone considers far fewer combinations than a search over classical plus quantum columns, and the wider search has more room to fit the inner criterion whatever the extra columns contain. `selected_nystroem` exists to remove that asymmetry: the same channels plus a classical kernel expansion of the same width as the quantum block. Read the quantum result against THAT arm, not only against `selected_raw`.",
    "no_quantum_advantage_claim": "No quantum-advantage claim. The quantum columns are a deterministic function of the classical channels, so the data-processing inequality applies. One extraction at a finite shot budget does not bound extraction-seed variance; the fold protocol handles split variance only.",
    "synthetic_data": "The demo dataset is synthetic and authored so that a linear detector needs an interaction term. That is a property of the dataset, not a measurement of the world."
  }
}